<Project>

  <!--
    Shared MSBuild settings for every project in the repository (§4.1). The
    target framework lives here rather than in each csproj because it is a
    platform-wide decision: .NET 10 (LTS) and C# 14, settled in CLAUDE.md and
    not a per-project choice.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Target">
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>14.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Build output leaves the source trees. Without this the SDK writes `bin/` and
    `obj/` beside every `.csproj`, so `src/` and `tests/` hold two things at
    once: the files a reader is looking for, and everything a build put there.
    One `artifacts/` directory at the repository root holds all of it instead,
    in the shape §4.1 draws, and `src/` is then what its name says: code,
    project files and the prose beside them.

    `UseArtifactsOutput` has to be set here rather than in a project, and the
    build says so rather than shrugging. The SDK computes the output paths
    before a project's own properties are imported, so the same two lines in a
    `.csproj` arrive too late to move the intermediate output — and rather than
    ignore them it fails the build with **NETSDK1199**, on the argument that a
    path silently depending on whether a `Directory.Build.props` happened to
    exist would be worse than an error. `Directory.Build.props` and the command
    line are the two places early enough, which is why they are here and why a
    new project needs no line at all.

    `ArtifactsPath` is spelled out rather than left to its default. The default
    derives from whichever `Directory.Build.props` set `UseArtifactsOutput`,
    which is this one, so the value is the same; saying it makes the location a
    decision in the file that owns it rather than a fact a reader has to know
    the SDK's search order to predict. `MSBuildThisFileDirectory` carries its
    own trailing separator, so `artifacts` appends directly.

    Two lines and no third, and the third is the one worth writing down.
    `UseArtifactsOutput` moves `MSBuildProjectExtensionsPath` as well as the
    intermediate output — measured, not inferred from the name — so
    `project.assets.json` and the generated `.nuget.g.props` land under
    `artifacts/obj/<Project>/` too and a restore leaves nothing beside a
    `.csproj` either. Setting that property here would restate a path the SDK
    already derives, in a second place that can disagree with the first: NuGet
    resolves it at restore time and again at build time, and a mismatch fails
    the build on an assets file it cannot find.

    What it would NOT do is make the outcome any more certain, which is the
    whole of the argument. The property is the SDK's default and not this
    file's decision, so the thing to hold is the result rather than the
    setting: `.github/output-gate/` fails CI on the day a build writes into
    `src/` or `tests/` again, whichever default moved underneath it.

    Consumers outside MSBuild depend on this path, and each argues its own case
    in the file that holds it: `.gitignore` and `.dockerignore` exclude
    `artifacts/`, and `.github/secret-scan/secret_scan.py` declines to walk it.
    Naming them here is a pointer to where the reasons live, not a second copy
    of them — a list that is a copy goes stale on the next consumer, and a
    reader who trusts it never opens the file that is right.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Output">
    <UseArtifactsOutput>true</UseArtifactsOutput>
    <ArtifactsPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)artifacts</ArtifactsPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The analyser policy of ADR-019. TreatWarningsAsErrors is what makes the
    other two lines mean anything: without it a violation is a message in a
    build log nobody reads, and the log is longest on the pull request that
    introduced the most of them.

    EnforceCodeStyleInBuild promotes the .editorconfig from an editor
    convenience to a build input — but only for rules configured at warning or
    above. Three are, and .editorconfig says which and why. The rest stay
    suggestions deliberately; a style rule with a documented exception it cannot
    express is a rule that must not fail a build.

    latest-Recommended rather than latest-All: the All mode turns on every
    CAxxxx analyser including the documentation and globalisation categories,
    which produce hundreds of diagnostics on a codebase that has made no
    commitment to either.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Analysis">
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-Recommended</AnalysisLevel>

    <!--
      CA1716 flags a type or member whose name is a reserved word in another
      .NET language, so that a VB or F# consumer does not have to escape it. It
      fires on `Error` (§10.5), and would fire again on any of the short,
      obvious names a domain model reaches for.

      The rule protects a scenario this platform does not have. Nothing here is
      a published library: every project is consumed by C# inside this
      repository, and §4.3 permits exactly one assembly to cross a service
      boundary at all. Renaming `Error` to satisfy a VB caller that will never
      exist would cost the one name the whole error catalogue is written
      around.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1716</NoWarn>

    <!--
      CA1711 bans a reserved suffix on a type name, so that a consumer is not
      misled about what the type derives from. It fires on `NextDelegate`
      (§6.2), where the suffix is not incorrect at all: the type is a delegate,
      and saying so is the whole of the name.

      This is the CA1716 argument again, and it is admitted on the same terms.
      Both rules protect a consumer of a published library from a name they
      cannot change, and there is no such consumer here — every project is
      referenced by C# inside this repository, and §4.3 permits exactly one
      assembly to cross a service boundary.

      What it costs is real and worth writing down: the rule is off everywhere,
      so a later `OrderCollection` that is not a collection stops being caught.
      That is the trade this file is the right place to make, because it is a
      decision about the whole corpus rather than about one type.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1711</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    This file is the only place ADR-019 permits a suppression at all, `#pragma`
    being forbidden; each one's argument sits beside its own `NoWarn` line
    rather than being counted from here. CA1707 bans underscores in member
    names, and every test name in §12 is a sentence written with them —
    `Cannot_cancel_an_order_that_has_shipped`. The two rules are in direct
    conflict and one of them has to give.

    It is the analyser that gives, because the convention is load-bearing: a
    test name is read in a failure report by someone who is not looking at the
    code, and `CannotCancelAnOrderThatHasShipped` is worse at that job. Scoped
    to test projects by name, so a `_`-laden member in production code still
    fails the build.

    EndsWith('Tests') rather than '.Tests': Platform.IntegrationTests (§12.6)
    ends with the word and not the dotted suffix, and would otherwise be the one
    test project the carve-out missed.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Test analysis" Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('Tests'))">
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deterministic builds everywhere; path normalisation only on CI, because
    ContinuousIntegrationBuild rewrites source paths in the PDB to be
    repository-relative and that breaks local step-through debugging. The CI
    environment variable is set by GitHub Actions on every runner.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Build">
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
